,"begin":[1077,1],"end":[1077,77]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[{"other":"#[rustc_inherit_overflow_checks]"},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["self",{"borrowed_ref":{"lifetime":null,"is_mutable":true,"type":{"generic":"Self"}}}],["other",{"primitive":"u8"}]],"output":null,"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"42939":{"id":42939,"crate_id":0,"name":"vrndq_f32","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\..\\..\\stdarch\\crates\\core_arch\\src\\aarch64\\neon\\generated.rs","begin":[22966,1],"end":[22968,2]},"visibility":"public","docs":"Floating-point round to integral, toward zero\n[Arm's documentation](https://developer.arm.com/architectures/instruction-sets/intrinsics/vrndq_f32)","links":{},"attrs":[{"other":"#[<cfg_attr>(test, assert_instr(frintz))]"},{"other":"#[attr = Stability {stability: Stability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 59, patch: 0})}, feature: \"neon_intrinsics\"}}]"},{"target_feature":{"enable":["neon"]}},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["a",{"resolved_path":{"path":"float32x4_t","id":9019,"args":null}}]],"output":{"resolved_path":{"path":"float32x4_t","id":9019,"args":null}},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"3194":{"id":3194,"crate_id":0,"name":"Output","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\num\\saturating.rs","begin":[551,1],"end":[551,73]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"assoc_type":{"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"bounds":[],"type":{"qualified_path":{"name":"Output","args":null,"self_type":{"resolved_path":{"path":"Saturating","id":1953,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"primitive":"u128"}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"trait":{"path":"BitXor","id":2357,"args":null}}}}}},"24992":{"id":24992,"crate_id":0,"name":"collect","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\iter\\traits\\iterator.rs","begin":[2014,5],"end":[2028,6]},"visibility":"default","docs":"Transforms an iterator into a collection.\n\n`collect()` can take anything iterable, and turn it into a relevant\ncollection. This is one of the more powerful methods in the standard\nlibrary, used in a variety of contexts.\n\nThe most basic pattern in which `collect()` is used is to turn one\ncollection into another. You take a collection, call [`iter`] on it,\ndo a bunch of transformations, and then `collect()` at the end.\n\n`collect()` can also create instances of types that are not typical\ncollections. For example, a [`String`] can be built from [`char`]s,\nand an iterator of [`Result<T, E>`][`Result`] items can be collected\ninto `Result<Collection<T>, E>`. See the examples below for more.\n\nBecause `collect()` is so general, it can cause problems with type\ninference. As such, `collect()` is one of the few times you'll see\nthe syntax affectionately known as the 'turbofish': `::<>`. This\nhelps the inference algorithm understand specifically which collection\nyou're trying to collect into.\n\n# Examples\n\nBasic usage:\n\n```\nlet a = [1, 2, 3];\n\nlet doubled: Vec<i32> = a.iter()\n                         .map(|x| x * 2)\n                         .collect();\n\nassert_eq!(vec![2, 4, 6], doubled);\n```\n\nNote that we needed the `: Vec<i32>` on the left-hand side. This is because\nwe could collect into, for example, a [`VecDeque<T>`] instead:\n\n[`VecDeque<T>`]: ../../std/collections/struct.VecDeque.html\n\n```\nuse std::collections::VecDeque;\n\nlet a = [1, 2, 3];\n\nlet doubled: VecDeque<i32> = a.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect();\n\nassert_eq!(2, doubled[0]);\nassert_eq!(4, doubled[1]);\nassert_eq!(6, doubled[2]);\n```\n\nUsing the 'turbofish' instead of annotating `doubled`:\n\n```\nlet a = [1, 2, 3];\n\nlet doubled = a.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect::<Vec<i32>>();\n\nassert_eq!(vec![2, 4, 6], doubled);\n```\n\nBecause `collect()` only cares about what you're collecting into, you can\nstill use a partial type hint, `_`, with the turbofish:\n\n```\nlet a = [1, 2, 3];\n\nlet doubled = a.iter().map(|x| x * 2).collect::<Vec<_>>();\n\nassert_eq!(vec![2, 4, 6], doubled);\n```\n\nUsing `collect()` to make a [`String`]:\n\n```\nlet chars = ['g', 'd', 'k', 'k', 'n'];\n\nlet hello: String = chars.into_iter()\n    .map(|x| x as u8)\n    .map(|x| (x + 1) as char)\n    .collect();\n\nassert_eq!(\"hello\", hello);\n```\n\nIf you have a list of [`Result<T, E>`][`Result`]s, you can use `collect()` to\nsee if any of them failed:\n\n```\nlet results = [Ok(1), Err(\"nope\"), Ok(3), Err(\"bad\")];\n\nlet result: Result<Vec<_>, &str> = results.into_iter().collect();\n\n// gives us the first error\nassert_eq!(Err(\"nope\"), result);\n\nlet results = [Ok(1), Ok(3)];\n\nlet result: Result<Vec<_>, &str> = results.into_iter().collect();\n\n// gives us the list of answers\nassert_eq!(Ok(vec![1, 3]), result);\n```\n\n[`iter`]: Iterator::next\n[`String`]: ../../std/string/struct.String.html\n[`char`]: type@char","links":{"`Result`":85,"Iterator::next":23699,"type@char":10615},"attrs":[{"other":"#[rustc_diagnostic_item = \"iterator_collect_fn\"]"},{"other":"#[attr = Stability {stability: Stability {level: Stable {since: Version(RustcVersion {major: 1,\nminor: 0, patch: 0})}, feature: \"rust1\"}}]"},{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"},{"must_use":{"reason":"if you really need to exhaust the iterator, consider `.for_each(drop)` instead"}}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["self",{"generic":"Self"}]],"output":{"generic":"B"},"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[{"name":"B","kind":{"type":{"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"FromIterator","id":161,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"qualified_path":{"name":"Item","args":null,"self_type":{"generic":"Self"},"trait":{"path":"","id":77,"args":null}}}}],"constraints":[]}}},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"default":null,"is_synthetic":false}}}],"where_predicates":[{"bound_predicate":{"type":{"generic":"Self"},"bounds":[{"trait_bound":{"trait":{"path":"Sized","id":7,"args":null},"generic_params":[],"modifier":"none"}}],"generic_params":[]}}]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has_body":true}}},"46790":{"id":46790,"crate_id":0,"name":"mask16x4","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\..\\..\\portable-simd\\crates\\core_simd\\src\\alias.rs","begin":[177,1],"end":[227,2]},"visibility":"public","docs":"A SIMD mask with four elements for vectors with 16-bit element types.\n\nThe layout of this type is unspecified, and may change between platforms and/or Rust versions, and code should not assume that it is equivalent to `[i16; 4]`.","links":{},"attrs":[{"other":"#[allow(non_camel_case_types)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"type_alias":{"type":{"resolved_path":{"path":"$crate::simd::Mask","id":9458,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"primitive":"i16"}},{"const":{"expr":"$num_elements","value":null,"is_literal":true}}],"constraints":[]}}}},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]}}}},"7045":{"id":7045,"crate_id":0,"name":"bitor_assign","span":{"filename":"library\\core\\src\\num\\wrapping.rs","begin":[566,1],"end":[566,71]},"visibility":"default","docs":null,"links":{},"attrs":[{"other":"#[attr = Inline(Hint)]"}],"deprecation":null,"inner":{"function":{"sig":{"inputs":[["self",{"borrowed_ref":{"lifetime":null,"is_mutable":true,"type":{"generic":"Self"}}}],["other",{"resolved_path":{"path":"Wrapping","id":4333,"args":{"angle_bracketed":{"args":[{"type":{"primitive":"i128"}}],"constraints":[]}}}}]],"output":null,"is_c_variadic":false},"generics":{"params":[],"where_predicates":[]},"header":{"is_const":false,"is_unsafe":false,"is_async":false,"abi":"Rust"},"has